# 📊 Data Jobs Dashboard

An interactive **Power BI dashboard for analyzing the data-job market**, focusing on job demand, salaries, job trends, work-from-home opportunities, degree requirements, benefits, job platforms, employment types, and geographic distribution.

The dashboard allows users to explore the job market at a high level and then **drill through into individual job titles** for a more detailed analysis.

---

## ✨ Highlights

- 📈 Analyze **data-job demand and posting trends**
- 💰 Compare **median yearly and hourly salaries**
- ⭐ Analyze **average job ratings**
- 🔎 Filter the dashboard by **job title**
- 🏠 Compare **work-from-home opportunities**
- 🎓 Analyze whether job postings mention a **degree requirement**
- 🏥 Explore **health insurance availability**
- 🌐 Compare job postings across **job platforms**
- 💼 Analyze different **employment types**
- 🌍 Explore job demand and salaries by **country**
- 🔄 Drill through from the main dashboard into a **specific job title**

---

## 🖥️ Dashboard Pages

### 1. 📊 Data Jobs Dashboard

The main dashboard provides a high-level overview of the data-job market.

#### Key KPIs

The dashboard displays:

| KPI | Description |
|---|---|
| **Job Count** | Total number of job postings |
| **Median Yearly Salary** | Median annual salary across job postings |
| **Median Hourly Salary** | Median hourly salary |
| **Average Job Rating** | Average rating associated with job postings |

A **Job Title slicer** allows the user to filter the dashboard and investigate specific roles.

---

### 📈 Job Market Overview

The dashboard compares different job titles based on:

- Number of job postings
- Median yearly salary
- Median hourly salary

A **scatter chart** is used to visualize the relationship between job demand and compensation.

This makes it easier to identify roles that combine:

- High job demand
- High salaries
- Lower or higher compensation relative to other roles

---

### 📊 Job Title Comparison

A bar chart compares the number of postings across different job titles.

This provides a quick view of which data-related roles have the greatest demand in the dataset.

---

### 📅 Job Posting Trends

A time-series visualization tracks job posting volume over time.

The dashboard supports date-based analysis across:

```text
Year
 └── Quarter
      └── Month
           └── Day
```

This makes it possible to investigate changes in job demand over time.

---

### 📋 Job Market Table

The dashboard also provides a detailed table containing:

- Job Title
- Salary Median
- Hourly Median
- Job Count
- Quarterly job trends

The table includes **sparklines** to provide a compact view of job-posting trends for each role.

---

## 🔎 Job Title Drill Through

The second page provides a detailed analysis for a selected job title.

Users can select a job title from the main dashboard and drill through to a dedicated analysis page.

For example:

```text
Data Jobs Dashboard
        ↓
Select Job Title
        ↓
Drill Through
        ↓
Detailed Job Title Analysis
```

---

## 💰 Salary Analysis

The drill-through page provides detailed salary statistics for the selected job title.

### Yearly Salary

The dashboard analyzes:

- Minimum yearly salary
- Maximum yearly salary
- Average yearly salary
- Median yearly salary

### Hourly Salary

It also provides:

- Minimum hourly salary
- Maximum hourly salary
- Average hourly salary
- Median hourly salary

This gives a more complete picture of compensation rather than relying on a single salary metric.

---

## 🏠 Work From Home

A donut chart shows the distribution of job postings that offer or mention:

**Work From Home**

This helps evaluate the availability of remote opportunities for a selected job title.

---

## 🎓 Degree Requirements

The dashboard analyzes whether job postings mention a **degree requirement**.

This is useful for understanding how educational requirements vary between different data-related careers.

---

## 🏥 Health Insurance

The dashboard also analyzes the availability/mention of **health insurance** in job postings.

This provides an additional perspective on the benefits associated with different roles.

---

## 🌐 Job Platforms

A bar chart compares job postings by the platform through which they were posted.

This helps identify the major sources of job opportunities for a selected role.

---

## 💼 Job Type

The dashboard breaks down jobs by employment type, including:

- Full-time
- Part-time
- Internship
- Contractor
- Temp work

A treemap is used to visualize the percentage distribution of these job types.

---

## 🌍 Geographic Analysis

The drill-through page includes a geographic map showing job opportunities by **country**.

The map provides:

- Country
- Job Count
- Median Yearly Salary

This makes it possible to investigate both **where jobs are concentrated** and **how compensation varies geographically**.

---

## 🧠 Dashboard Design

The dashboard follows a two-level analytical structure:

```text
                    DATA JOBS MARKET
                           │
             ┌─────────────┴─────────────┐
             │                           │
       Market Overview             Job Title Analysis
             │                           │
       ┌─────┼─────┐             ┌───────┼────────┐
       │     │     │             │       │        │
     Demand Salary Trends       Salary Benefits Geography
       │     │     │             │       │        │
       └─────┴─────┘             └───────┴────────┘
```

The goal is to allow users to move from a **general market overview** to a **specific job-level analysis**.

---

## 🗂️ Dashboard Structure

```text
Data Jobs Dashboard
│
├── 📊 Main Dashboard
│   ├── Job Count
│   ├── Median Yearly Salary
│   ├── Median Hourly Salary
│   ├── Average Job Rating
│   ├── Job Title Filter
│   ├── Salary vs Job Demand
│   ├── Job Title Comparison
│   ├── Job Posting Trends
│   └── Job Market Table
│
└── 🔎 Job Title Drill Through
    ├── Work From Home
    ├── Salary Analysis
    ├── Degree Requirement
    ├── Health Insurance
    ├── Job Platform
    ├── Employment Type
    └── Country Analysis
```

